import{_ as i,o as n,c as a,ak as t}from"./chunks/framework.B8jO0odJ.js";const u=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Chainy manuscript working draft","description":"Sean owns this drafting foundation, aligned with the current GDD.\\nUse the official outline and evidence requirements for the submitted manuscript's numbering and appendices.\\nC","frontmatter":{"title":"Chainy manuscript working draft","description":"Sean owns this drafting foundation, aligned with the current GDD.\\nUse the official outline and evidence requirements for the submitted manuscript's numbering and appendices.\\nC","source":"docs/manuscript-working-draft.md","category":"Research & evaluation","sourceUpdated":"2026-11-11T12:24:37+08:00","outline":[2,3]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"content/docs/manuscript-working-draft.md","filePath":"content/docs/manuscript-working-draft.md"}`),o={name:"content/docs/manuscript-working-draft.md"};function r(s,e,c,d,l,p){return n(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[t('<div><h1 id="chainy-manuscript-working-draft">Chainy manuscript working draft<a class="header-anchor" href="#chainy-manuscript-working-draft" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h1><p>Sean owns this drafting foundation, aligned with the <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/game-design-lesson-collection.html">current GDD</a>. Use the <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/manuscript-evidence-map.html">official outline and evidence requirements</a> for the submitted manuscript&#39;s numbering and appendices. <strong>Current work is documentation and preparation only; development requires final owner approval.</strong> Intended behavior below must not become a past-tense implementation or evaluation claim without evidence. The <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/archive/pre-lesson-collection-2026-11-11/manuscript-working-draft.html">previous draft</a> preserves the superseded design.</p><h2 id="chapter-i-introduction">Chapter I: introduction<a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-i-introduction" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><h3 id="project-context">Project context<a class="header-anchor" href="#project-context" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><p>Chainy is intended for learners aged 12–17, especially communities with limited connectivity. It introduces Web3, Blockchain and artificial intelligence through a single-player, English-first offline Android game. These audience and access priorities are project decisions; measured needs at a study site still require supporting evidence.</p><h3 id="purpose-and-description">Purpose and description<a class="header-anchor" href="#purpose-and-description" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><p>The proposed game takes the player from a main menu to Web3, Blockchain and AI islands in that order. A tutorial NPC introduces the mobile joystick, interactions, Encyclopedia, Quest and Pause. Each island has 13 lessons in prerequisite order. Thirty ordinary lessons use collectible objects, while nine lessons use distinct minigames in separate scenes, three per world. Minigames are reached through ordered item, NPC and facility interactions.</p><p>Completing a lesson records its explanation and example in the encyclopedia. All 13 entries open that world&#39;s required 13-question review, with one question per lesson. Players receive feedback and retry incorrect answers until every question is correct. Saved review completion finishes the world and unlocks the next island. SQLite is the local progress store, and AI activities are labeled offline teaching simulations.</p><h3 id="objectives">Objectives<a class="header-anchor" href="#objectives" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><p>The general objective is to design, develop and evaluate an offline educational game introducing the selected emerging-technology concepts.</p><p>Proposed specific objectives are to:</p><ul><li>Provide 39 sourced lessons with understandable examples, nine distinct practice activities and three required reviews.</li><li>Support ordered progress, encyclopedia collection and sequential world access with dependable local saves.</li><li>Verify the delivered game&#39;s offline operation, touch usability and recovery on the reference Android phone.</li><li>Evaluate the delivered system using the school&#39;s approved procedure and report supported findings and limitations.</li></ul><p>These are intended outcomes, not completed work or a promise of completion by the pre-final date.</p><h3 id="scope-and-delimitation">Scope and delimitation<a class="header-anchor" href="#scope-and-delimitation" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><p>The current design excludes an economy system, teacher services, exports, standalone Wordle and BlockMatch. Those earlier proposals are retained as history, not a later delivery commitment. The game requires no real wallet, financial transaction, public blockchain, cloud model or actual model training. A local profile identifies progress on one device; it does not authenticate enrollment or recover another phone&#39;s records. Device compatibility and educational effectiveness remain subject to actual verification.</p><h3 id="significance">Significance<a class="header-anchor" href="#significance" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><p>The intended contribution is an accessible learning experience with clear explanations, practice, feedback and dependable offline progress. Its educational value must be assessed from the delivered system and appropriate evidence, rather than inferred from collection or completion.</p><h2 id="chapter-ii-literature-and-technical-background">Chapter II: literature and technical background<a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-ii-literature-and-technical-background" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Build a verified review of game-based learning, introductory technology education, offline mobile access, feedback, usability and evaluation. For each study, record participants, method, findings, limitations and relevance. The <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/planning/a-z-complete-lesson-quiz-source-draft.html">lesson source pack</a> supplies topic provenance; its inclusion does not establish school curriculum approval.</p><p>Define a world as a subject area, a lesson as one recorded term, and a quest as its ordered interaction. Distinguish collection, corrected review completion, first-response performance and independent assessment. Use the <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/technical-design.html">technical design</a> and <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/design/database-schema.html">database reference</a> for proposed architecture and dated observations, not as proof of implemented behavior.</p><h2 id="chapter-iii-design-and-methodology">Chapter III: design and methodology<a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-iii-design-and-methodology" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>The <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/planning/lesson-collection-delivery.html">readiness plan</a> prepares bounded work and handoffs before final approval to develop. After authorization, implementation and verification should follow the approved dependencies and record actual outcomes. Unity supplies the game presentation and behavior; SQLite stores profile-specific checkpoints and quiz attempts. The design requires consistent lesson/encyclopedia updates, safe scene return and world unlocks only after successful review saving.</p><p>The reference phone is Android 10/API 29 with 4 GB physical RAM, 128 GB total storage and a reported 2.3 GHz octa-core CPU. “vivo 2019” is a supplied evidence label, not a verified retail-model identification. API 25 is the retained minimum install setting. ABI, graphics support, display characteristics and runtime performance require actual technical evidence. Do not publish device identifiers.</p><p>Research design, respondents, sampling, sample size, assistance policy, instruments and analysis remain for school/adviser confirmation. A pre/post procedure is only a proposal if the approved study measures learning change. Keep the school&#39;s supplied “ISO 2010” wording until the intended standard or instrument is identified. Use <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/qa-evaluation.html">QA and evaluation</a>, <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/design/app-flow.html">player flow</a> and <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/hardware-validation.html">hardware validation</a> as supporting references.</p><h2 id="chapters-ivv-results-and-conclusions">Chapters IV–V: results and conclusions<a class="header-anchor" href="#chapters-ivv-results-and-conclusions" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>No new results are supplied by this draft. Write preliminary evaluation, system evaluation and technical results only from actual records, identifying the build, participant context, usable and missing evidence, method and limitations. Keep raw participant records and identifying documents in the approved restricted location.</p><p>Conclusions must answer the objectives only to the extent supported by findings. Correction retries support game completion; they do not establish independent mastery or learning improvement. The November 10 session&#39;s actual outcome and its acceptance as school pilot evidence remain unknown. <strong>Pre-final remains November 21, 2026.</strong> Unfinished board dates will be rebaselined after documentation review; integration, device QA, manuscript and rehearsal still need capacity.</p></div>',1)])])}const m=i(o,[["render",r]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
